%S A401593 7,7,7,7,7,7,9,7,65,7,7,7,7,9,9,7,7,65,65,7,7,7,7,7,4095,7,
%T A401593 96570795929025857905165991126271791016959314530215915583,9,9,9,
%U A401593 6277101735386680763835789423207666416102355444464034512895,7,4097,7,7,65,65,65,3355443,7
%N A401593 Denominator of the 2-adic rational xi(h(n)), with h as in A401592.
%C A401593 The numerators are in A401592.
%C A401593 xi reads a power series over F_2 as a 2-adic integer, the coefficient of q^i as the binary digit of 2^i. Monks (2025) lists xi(h(n)) for n = 1..20 in Table 1; h(1) = P1 = (1+q)/(1+q^3), xi(P1) = -3/7, a point of the 2-cycle of T_q.
%C A401593 Every h(n) is rational: with s = A006666(n), m = A006667(n), and the odd steps of the T-orbit of n at positions k_1 < ... < k_m, h(n) = (q^s*P1 + Sum_{o=1..m} q^(k_o)*(1+q)^(m-o))/(1+q)^m, the q-analog of 3^m*n = 2^s - Sum_{o=1..m} 2^(k_o)*3^(m-o) with 2 -> q, 3 -> 1+q (no signs in characteristic 2). So the coefficients of h(n) are eventually periodic, and A401593(n) divides 2^e - 1, e the period. Since the denominator of h(n) divides (1+q)^m*(1+q+q^2), which divides (1+q^3)^(2^c) = 1 + q^(3*2^c) when 2^c >= m, the period e divides 3*2^c.
%H A401593 Ruud H.G. van Tol, <a href="/A401593/b401593.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A401593 Kenneth G. Monks, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.10153">On q-analogs of the 3x+1 Dynamical System</a>, J. Integer Sequences 28 (2025), Article 25.6.8 (Table 1); arXiv:2508.10153 [math.NT], 2025.
%H A401593 Ruud H.G. van Tol, <a href="https://github.com/druud/Collatz-but-stop-at-the-drop">Collatz, but stop at the drop</a>, Lean 4 formalization (modules TMonksDrop, TMonksXi).
%F A401593 a(2n) = a(n).
%F A401593 a(n) divides 2^(3*2^c) - 1 for any c with 2^c >= A006667(n).
%o A401593 (PARI) a(n) = denominator(xh(n)); \\ with xh(n) as in A401592.
%Y A401593 Cf. A006666, A006667, A014682, A389685 (the same for Monks' T_{1,1+q^2}, Table 2), A401592 (numerators).
